#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073435 :question/id #uuid "64a6782b-aa64-5027-8311-3498c19c39e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12549" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/5c/5ccfa3c27c6bb2759ffc37c73b3640583fa962ee79f47b21f02ac0c1d9164b00.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 5$ and passes through the point $(1,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-2x+2 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{2}x-5 \\\\ y=-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by setting the two equations equal and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =-2x+5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+2x & =5+5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $\\left( 4,-3\\right).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,3)$ and $\\left( 4, -3 \\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-1)}^{2}+{(-3-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ is $3 \\sqrt{5}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050975 :topic/math-academy-id 459 :topic/title "The Distance Formula"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecff5e98-4870-5c40-afc1-3cf86e73cc00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073305} {:answer/id #uuid "2abe9781-c4da-5b9a-ac9a-61ad46d56af1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073306} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8931d8-3921-5510-ad9d-3bf106f90ed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073307} {:answer/id #uuid "718f5aba-8b70-56a0-ab8e-3cbb131f7c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073308} {:answer/id #uuid "71c0cd20-495d-587c-8b24-54c3422ac0d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073309}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9ed308e3-6521-5514-84ca-2d2e05145ba5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073304}] :question/id #uuid "96761d25-edf9-59db-8b13-ca9096372133" :question/math-academy-id "q-74036" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1, 1)$ and the line $y =-\\frac{3}{4}x + 8$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8db9f448-bc9c-5bcb-86df-9f16d3725e1b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073311} {:answer/id #uuid "bd49f809-6093-563e-b332-e8ec4b8c58ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{30}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073312} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd90d1d-6e9f-519f-b6c9-165b2812ef98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073313} {:answer/id #uuid "215f9552-5bd7-500c-ad2e-523869c2d3fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073314} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8fe857-34e7-598b-8664-1b28dce0c373" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073315}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a0b36513-2904-598d-b0f6-b8bce75dd9da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186073310}] :question/id #uuid "b19c30c6-750d-572e-8955-3d513e59f868" :question/math-academy-id "q-74037" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1, 1)$ and the line $2y - x =-7$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234360 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1261027f-513a-5087-8c37-64cc20c45bd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74437" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(2,5)$ and the line $3y+2x=6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =6 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/2e/2e9224ad718acaea23e8e1b50ac0f4c30099710652db7528406ec9ec11baaecd.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2$ and passes through the point $(2,5).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+2 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =2-2 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=2.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(0,2).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(2,5)$ and $(0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0-2)}^{2}+(2-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(2,5)$ and the line $3y+2x=6$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47329e42-9fda-5dad-b1b7-b5de92199ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248057} {:answer/id #uuid "584e2ce7-69ed-515b-b3d8-ae953f17271d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248225} {:answer/id #uuid "90842d88-2e12-51f4-925b-763cbdc1a0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248708} {:answer/id #uuid "ad07dcb5-9bc9-5064-b6c1-d6071522fe76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248972} {:answer/id #uuid "df5f96e4-8268-56cf-9d0e-ec8e56b4e1d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47329e42-9fda-5dad-b1b7-b5de92199ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248057} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d7aa87d-cda1-557e-8fb5-e5baaeecced7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249813}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "447c75d4-22ec-5570-87c0-7a6b7bbeb6a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-114341" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](images/3a/3a4bbfd80480fe3fcc778a490514d27d65d83abf77ca1752e4203d9b3185b618.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(1,-5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+5 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{17}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{17}{3}-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{26}{3} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{26}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{6}{13}) \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(4,-3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,-5)$ and $(4,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-1)^{2}+(-3+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,-5)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc625ac-31af-5204-8aa1-d135161d7f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247552} {:answer/id #uuid "0ef14e40-61a3-5e9d-8570-f753f7cc4507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247562} {:answer/id #uuid "17d12124-8c76-5d6d-96b2-2686e53d0068" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247640} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6ebd32-522e-573e-b812-a47b4e052305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247836} {:answer/id #uuid "80085e2c-2862-5780-b8b7-faf2920e4807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248548}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ef14e40-61a3-5e9d-8570-f753f7cc4507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247562} :answer-field/id #uuid "54362198-2ea2-5313-8e36-1d82209afe8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249825}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "633d0fd6-e7a8-521b-a27a-54658471a6ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-114343" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/02/021b08ba0d14e40939103d6d3e9e2004d49e5d047a5f89677b3fe542aa866f82.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x-2$ and passes through the point $(-1,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =-(x+1) \\\\ y & =-x-1+5 \\\\ y & =-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x-2 \\\\ y=-x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =-x+4 \\\\ x+x & =4+2 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=1.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=1.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,1).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,5)$ and $(3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3+1)^{2}+(1-5)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,5)$ and the line $y=x-2$ is $4\\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250253 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40d864c7-ac82-5dbb-8af7-d444b0880713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247996} {:answer/id #uuid "64603703-91c7-5f97-9db2-b4074b474aae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248331} {:answer/id #uuid "b312cc92-9b6c-5e29-9985-1e5b5251db28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249028} {:answer/id #uuid "d0259228-442a-50fb-9be1-969be8149386" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249268} {:answer/id #uuid "fb00d5f7-9c6f-553e-84c5-26bcbcc3add2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249606}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40d864c7-ac82-5dbb-8af7-d444b0880713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247996} :answer-field/id #uuid "15ff26ed-290e-55e2-85c2-69f93ba8a5a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249707}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d7dd6e7-50c5-518a-95a2-582c9f1e9bcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114358" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+5x & =10 \\\\ 2y & =-5x+10 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{5}{2}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.  \n\n![](images/8c/8c4238a00b775d4db828651ae2691596bfadafcdc748494cc957bad1cb1b6b91.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5$ and passes through the point $(-5,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}(x+5) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{5}x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}x+5 & =\\frac{2}{5}x+5 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x-\\frac{2}{5}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{29}{10}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=5.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(0,5).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-5,3)$ and $(0,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(0-(-5))}^{2}+(5-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-5,3)$ and the line $2y+5x=10$ is $\\sqrt{29}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192f0711-d55c-54c1-ac3b-10bbb9d90325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247650} {:answer/id #uuid "2420a00b-8993-51c9-a7ec-8f593f1087a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247739} {:answer/id #uuid "55c77c69-7f9d-53ec-bc57-775c7a8eb546" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248199} {:answer/id #uuid "570c35db-46b1-5a19-9b59-605bcf9b8053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248213} {:answer/id #uuid "8b235cea-ca61-5478-8e9a-f1b19691f30b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "570c35db-46b1-5a19-9b59-605bcf9b8053" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248213} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8a356fc-a06e-5ab6-aa77-918e09dee889" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249990}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9130ee0d-24eb-5b04-a0dc-d2670f4f9892" :question/math-academy-id "q-114364" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y+3x & =-6 \\\\ 2y & =-3x-6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/71/715e1292edaf78979f6b9ae36586a8196dcc30e865915e7eefba2360281f85ec.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3$ and passes through the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x-3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{5}{6} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{5}{6}+3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{3}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(-1-2)}^{2}+{(-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(2,\\frac{1}{2})$ and the line $2y+3x=-6$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b6fecf3-af6d-570c-a4e0-5bd2f8943b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339805} {:answer/id #uuid "86531e02-9d87-59a8-99d4-328fa2c86fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340333} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e737ba-76b9-5760-80fc-271f650d20e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341023} {:answer/id #uuid "e47935d1-86c8-5a85-a24b-c428d0150116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341149} {:answer/id #uuid "e58979f5-86fa-57ab-b8b7-766696bc055b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "86531e02-9d87-59a8-99d4-328fa2c86fbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340333} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f8851fb-c592-5f14-9bf4-a48b2c458a74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341574}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f0e530ca-0510-53a9-a606-7cbe24765900" :question/math-academy-id "q-74417" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/eb/eb1176b4a5e2565fceb03e9af652a6ae875f5ee678c103405045fcebc36342e1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x+2$ and passes through the point $(-1,-3).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+3 & =1(x+1) \\\\ y+3 & =x+1 \\\\ y & =x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-x+2 \\\\ y=x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+2 & =x-2 \\\\ -x-x & =-2-2 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-1,-3)$ and $(2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-(-1))^{2}+(0-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-3)$ and the line $y=-x+2$ is $3\\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19034fbf-ecd9-55d6-861b-0d5e5661524d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339349} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddbc882-a5c7-5c65-8d64-d4d3043edf48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339533} {:answer/id #uuid "4add45f8-1363-5fe9-be2e-64e9fe43e788" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339801} {:answer/id #uuid "83bfa442-6fa5-5f09-aec0-57fb97c3556e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340313} {:answer/id #uuid "b1385a23-f178-5808-937d-bee3d508b96e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ddbc882-a5c7-5c65-8d64-d4d3043edf48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339533} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f5e6af3-06a0-5b37-9c66-995c74e7a84d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341663}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac99e412-cc1f-5160-a402-dce6424f38c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-74420" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/6e/6ec04fe0ad996f87dbc07b0883afb3fa7aef1429412a550afe9102d1c22db71b.png)\n\n  \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ and passes through the point $(5,-8).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{1}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-3.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+8 & =-3(x-5) \\\\ y+8 & =-3x+15 \\\\ y & =-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3 \\\\ y=-3x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-3 & =-3x+7 \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =7+3 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,-2).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(5,-8)$ and $(3,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3-5)^{2}+(-2-(-8))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(5,-8)$ and the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3$ is $2\\sqrt{10}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342211 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cdc61b3-8b79-5ea8-a619-8426af491629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339670} {:answer/id #uuid "67f37f95-6de1-5d04-b71e-ed84f5ca8a57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340056} {:answer/id #uuid "ab06bd68-2d98-5de6-8d36-5e85b3672d7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340684} {:answer/id #uuid "c3ad83ea-b045-52c1-bf05-b58e0937ea33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340869} {:answer/id #uuid "e8141639-7376-52d2-962a-0f9bff8db53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8141639-7376-52d2-962a-0f9bff8db53d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341176} :answer-field/id #uuid "e40ef719-3a96-5536-9367-e13d22ca4b80" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341785}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a03ec8c-4418-5e4a-aaf2-129c88104ba7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74435" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x & =-2 \\\\ y & =-2x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/78/789659bd700bc5f8407d409bc28bf5ce5309369af2aa5ad5973434d47cff74fa.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-2x-2$ and passes through the point $(-3,-6).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x+3) \\\\ y+6 & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2x-2 \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x-2 & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -2x-\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-4.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=-4.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(1,-4).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-3,-6)$ and $(1,-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(1-(-3))}^{2}+(-4-(-6))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-3,-6)$ and the line $y+2x=-2$ is $2\\sqrt{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cae1362-ea07-5824-927c-25a4a487e098" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484375} {:answer/id #uuid "7753ff6b-adb7-52b1-9810-10a100856790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484376} {:answer/id #uuid "7b72658c-4203-56e3-b013-04b826bee95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484378} {:answer/id #uuid "d014d8ac-e7ad-5b0e-8559-0c2bd1320040" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484385} {:answer/id #uuid "e714989d-5994-5fab-b070-da6b02739606" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7753ff6b-adb7-52b1-9810-10a100856790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484376} :answer-field/id #uuid "f52397e8-481d-5682-a1bc-10148c8b79d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484389}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "99d5220d-0d33-5926-9ea0-c7324f7866f5" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-3,15)$ and the line $y=x -6.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/c2/c2a38b553533a53e06a32db62890e7c91cf5c4f827a14a9fe9028dc18c399690.png)\n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x -6$ and passes through the point $(-3,15).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(15) & =-1(x-(-3)) \\\\ y & =12 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x -6 \\\\ y=12 -x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x -6 & =12 -x \\\\ (2)x & =18 \\\\ x & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=9$ into either of the two equations gives $y=3.$ So, our solution is $x=9$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(9,3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-3,15)$ and $(9,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{(9-(-3))^2+(3-(15))^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{144+144} \\\\ & =12 \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-3,15)$ and the line $y=x -6$ is $12 \\sqrt{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484383 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166e3f8c-f04b-5da1-9412-38ad97850cf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484372} {:answer/id #uuid "25df8fdd-3126-5e6d-8151-85d7400b86ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484373} {:answer/id #uuid "4922448d-3df4-5380-9f15-02e91d6305ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484374} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a88fdc-c029-5c95-a46c-5983a3cf3b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484386} {:answer/id #uuid "dfec296f-fcca-599e-821f-c9e9985dc17e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4922448d-3df4-5380-9f15-02e91d6305ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 \\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484374} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad3073cb-e9f3-5964-919f-4f70383397a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484381}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c182161a-7f52-5240-b03d-e600d3d24498" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(14,\\frac{7}{2})$ and the line $3 x + 2 y=-42.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 x + 2 y & =-42 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/bb/bbd49aa51f1721cb274e2fe4451aaec3319884eff891165fe0a9e941b04b4a70.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21$ and passes through the point $(14,\\frac{7}{2}).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-(14)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21 \\\\ y=\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3 x}{2} -21 & =\\frac{2 x}{3} -\\frac{35}{6} \\\\ (-\\frac{13}{6})x & =\\frac{91}{6} \\\\ x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-7$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-\\frac{21}{2}.$ So, our solution is $x=-7$ and $y=-\\frac{21}{2}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-7,-\\frac{21}{2}).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(14,\\frac{7}{2})$ and $(-7,-\\frac{21}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{(-7-(14))^2+(-\\frac{21}{2}-(\\frac{7}{2}))^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{441+196} \\\\ & =7 \\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(14,\\frac{7}{2})$ and the line $3 x + 2 y=-42$ is $7 \\sqrt{13}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186484390 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a40b10b-7f8f-567c-b059-d1778d42ed93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484370} {:answer/id #uuid "1436d41a-008b-5d24-943f-6c6ada0e47b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484371} {:answer/id #uuid "7a63166c-3fbf-5cf1-ad6c-ccc4fd6560b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484377} {:answer/id #uuid "bf167359-bd5b-561f-83d8-a12dda3667ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484382} {:answer/id #uuid "cb5cffb6-f40b-53a8-ba11-c1f8f059b0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484384}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb5cffb6-f40b-53a8-ba11-c1f8f059b0ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484384} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3d1ccc5-b072-55cb-987e-f64512327d14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484380}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f9e40c34-20e6-50f1-adaa-a0e86b153a34" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-15,9)$ and the line $5 x + 2 y=30.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 x + 2 y & =30 \\\\ y & =15 -\\frac{5 x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's draw a diagram describing our situation.\n\n![](images/ee/ee6ae236d06003f7c77eb6bf3f9e6c4ce1c317a9e08c9facf04b0a7f8065f9e2.png)\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=15 -\\frac{5 x}{2}$ and passes through the point $(-15,9).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{5}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{5}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 & =m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(9) & =\\frac{2}{5}(x-(-15)) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2 x}{5} + 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=15 -\\frac{5 x}{2} \\\\ y=\\frac{2 x}{5} + 15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 -\\frac{5 x}{2} & =\\frac{2 x}{5} + 15 \\\\ (-\\frac{29}{10})x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=15.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=15.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,15).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(-15,9)$ and $(0,15):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{(0-(-15))^2+(15-(9))^2} \\\\ & =\\sqrt{225+36} \\\\ & =3 \\sqrt{29}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(-15,9)$ and the line $5 x + 2 y=30$ is $3 \\sqrt{29}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073436 :question/id #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12612" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c48fc163b3c9543dcfdd03b20882f0d2a19747672c6b1db2a42a683f0014fe25.png)\n\n \n\nFind the point on the line $y-x=4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x & =4 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y =  x + 4$ and passes through the point $(3,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-1(x-3) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+3 \\\\ y & =-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=x+4 \\\\ y=-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =-x+6 \\\\ x+x & =6-4 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=5.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y-x= 4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3)$ is  $(1,5).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049869 :topic/math-academy-id 408 :topic/title "Calculating the Intersection of Two Lines"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473 :topic/math-academy-id 3562 :topic/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0498568d-b327-5f78-89e8-8587c0368b6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339162} {:answer/id #uuid "850104ea-f24f-5eb3-bc01-1f1701a3ca6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340323} {:answer/id #uuid "a884e107-b09a-542c-94ff-f2323802311a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340660} {:answer/id #uuid "c8516292-870e-5d6a-9fc2-73422fa2795d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340902} {:answer/id #uuid "d93f1851-2a54-53fb-9fee-5fb53f3f5e43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a884e107-b09a-542c-94ff-f2323802311a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340660} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1068806-46ea-595d-a611-e82e67e43211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341781}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2a5b92a-6d2c-561b-999b-9dedaf72311f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74060" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f97972b1765e58a76475a99a97e4a8818b05efa1f26a79a232b250b6ef681e9b.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=0.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is  $(-1,0).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6304ff82-aa6c-50b7-b79e-a11e972b7aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340009} {:answer/id #uuid "7d839962-7657-5bb7-904b-97d127c09ca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{9}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340259} {:answer/id #uuid "a38a60c9-c99e-5333-a142-a09672caa6e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340611} {:answer/id #uuid "befaa2f8-684a-5c95-bb90-4e92f8bb8e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340830} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc6bc70-eb00-5071-8312-8709fa64cf86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6304ff82-aa6c-50b7-b79e-a11e972b7aa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340009} :answer-field/id #uuid "f952c9dc-1256-5a8a-9688-3f9bfd070213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341826}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "853cfa3c-da08-562e-b7e9-24b69cddbfcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-74061" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d478fb4499ee19a7c385ae93dd5ad790404351ceda63c20341b9996a1466ca60.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1)$ is $(0,-2).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250245 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "321312ca-35e6-590b-b440-c12b3be53dcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{66}{13},-\\frac{18}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247881} {:answer/id #uuid "73f9e2ae-8b3b-58ab-9adc-90853b9cafce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{90}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248444} {:answer/id #uuid "7bcc3641-ada3-55ad-bd99-688a6222a88b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248510} {:answer/id #uuid "9bb2bdd5-d7c7-5d30-bb92-8a47f30a62f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},-\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248809} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f270dd-8b9b-593e-b900-f2f6c240780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8f270dd-8b9b-593e-b900-f2f6c240780a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249594} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d014b53-8f67-50f0-a715-6786d9eb85c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249691}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1e7059ea-e4f5-5468-9ed3-af001f239b6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114336" :question/problem "![](images/69/69e06e1b9b72e3a4c1fc2e7f2fcd85aa4aa8b34e4fa2f4f5aebb8d1b10474415.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ and passes through the point $(-3,4).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x+3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2+4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 \\\\ y=\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}x+3 & =\\frac{2}{3}x+6 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{2}{3}x & =6-3 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{18}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{18}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{66}{13}.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{3}{2}x+3$ that is closest to the point $(-3,4)$ is $(-\\frac{18}{13},\\frac{66}{13}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247490} {:answer/id #uuid "14581a73-ac53-505c-8447-0394a875fcd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},-\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247608} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb5a940-7e41-5de0-9f76-33adbc3d81da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248386} {:answer/id #uuid "bd6d5bb5-c38f-58f9-bdcf-296b1625b1d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{11}{13},\\frac{3}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249109} {:answer/id #uuid "de549bfd-d213-57c1-ad88-fadc596d833b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249395}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072e45bc-d35a-54a2-8ab9-5f295ebf110f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247490} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5d3e168-c279-50d0-a119-0930eb63b6db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249960}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd8e8e07-0b88-58f7-806a-13412093afb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114345" :question/problem "![](images/ff/ff9810290868f7fd465bc310c969255b92f646d6b59ad71a93a3dcc9c38ef49c.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y+2x & =3 \\\\ 3y & =-2x+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,5).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-5 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{9}{2}+5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =\\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{3}{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{3}{13}$ and $y=\\frac{11}{13}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $3y+2x=3$ that is closest to the point $(3,5)$ is  $(\\frac{3}{13},\\frac{11}{13}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bf8bb55-4020-5f0b-bd59-42ee32fbb771" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{7}{5},\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247677} {:answer/id #uuid "506a9fe3-70ee-5719-9e0a-dcce648d0473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248144} {:answer/id #uuid "9cf2ce60-0e78-5b08-b8b7-0a60d6e30d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248821} {:answer/id #uuid "bb02feef-1bc2-517b-96ff-29402a331b44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249094} {:answer/id #uuid "dbb3d233-8364-50ab-b259-e0a0966e7b9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},-\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249364}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cf2ce60-0e78-5b08-b8b7-0a60d6e30d7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248821} :answer-field/id #uuid "2932adcc-4eb8-504c-836b-faa4fbe63c1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249750}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3954acb1-fda8-5e85-a442-60c3272bf340" :question/math-academy-id "q-114346" :question/problem "![](images/df/df3838ae44d1477a4a0de303972cd26a2ceaad9b213f1d8e4be3a53bd453aeb2.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3x & =2 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=3x+2$ and passes through the point $(4,0).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $3,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-4) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=3x+2 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 3x+\\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{4}{3}-2 \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}x & =-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 10x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{7}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $y-3x=2$ that is closest to the point $(4,0)$ is  $(-\\frac{1}{5},\\frac{7}{5}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce54910-1244-5386-86d8-f09d91856276" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247960} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6a67d4-b82b-5092-bade-43eff2cd1bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248536} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e5e077-c051-52e8-9324-38476f689c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249076} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ca5e2c-1d09-5ba6-bac7-e091b9182a80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{9}{5},\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249285} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e2987c-b486-55c8-9dc4-132ab31c0731" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},-\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249450}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e6a67d4-b82b-5092-bade-43eff2cd1bd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248536} :answer-field/id #uuid "c816ccdc-152d-585a-8fbf-41891b81689b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250013}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "855df716-3231-50d2-97cf-6767626cecd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-114349" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba46b4f3f458a6be13ea01ca1351658c102732db94e22725890afaaade6fc904.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-10x & =5 \\\\ 5y & =10x+5 \\\\ y & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=2x+1$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $2,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x+2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x-1+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=2x+1 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+2 \\\\ 2x+\\frac{1}{2}x & =2-1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ into either of the two equations gives $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, our solution is $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ and $y=\\frac{9}{5}.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line  $5y-10x=5$ that is closest to the point $(-2,3)$ is  $(\\frac{2}{5},\\frac{9}{5}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "897863c7-0053-5881-8309-0bafe6422e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340355} {:answer/id #uuid "a9eb5854-49b2-5f3d-8f1a-f3ede7741417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{15}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340676} {:answer/id #uuid "b538ac87-ef4c-5ab5-bea9-11f7323e59a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340772} {:answer/id #uuid "c7070e71-630e-52cf-86b9-7b5b1c88b417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,-\\frac{13}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340891} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c949a5-d093-5c67-b8fe-bda979b82d09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{29}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "897863c7-0053-5881-8309-0bafe6422e75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340355} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ed75246-b3cc-5428-993e-e7cf98157952" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341493}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a7a18e4a-49f2-563e-8c4d-e70b3f9189cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-74421" :question/problem "![](images/67/67d09cf1ebb51a37c122ea1e0bb53b0bcabedca0251ae4105ebb93dd55d111dd.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ and passes through the point $(-4,-1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8 \\\\ y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-8 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =8-\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =\\frac{13}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{13}{3}\\cdot \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-5.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=-5.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,-5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=\\frac{3}{2}x-8$ that is closest to the point $(-4,-1)$ is $(2,-5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342212 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47f7ce0c-16e5-531a-94b9-547622df3f25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339767} {:answer/id #uuid "569cbb0a-f99c-5355-8f53-bdc678082993" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339923} {:answer/id #uuid "8688ee8a-8219-5c84-a042-632559c1a6df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340337} {:answer/id #uuid "95f977c5-80d6-5f10-bfe2-b1560cf968b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340481} {:answer/id #uuid "ae765e2d-062d-5b17-8c52-1eef7d4ff32e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-5,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95f977c5-80d6-5f10-bfe2-b1560cf968b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340481} :answer-field/id #uuid "73585de0-1b70-548d-9d76-12f3a61d673b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341582}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7220b7e3-9cce-5102-874c-014511b9e7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-74439" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d687bcdc23c283e0945b039781450d2a020f0fd89c0d2976bb252914b296978.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+x & =-4 \\\\ y & =-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-x-4$ and passes through the point $(0,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-2 & =x \\\\ y & =x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+2 \\\\ y=-x-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =-x-4 \\\\ x+x & =-4-2 \\\\ 2x & =-6 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-1.$ So, our solution is $x=-3$ and $y=-1.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-3,-1).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y+x=-4$ that is closest to the point $(0,2)$ is  $(-3,-1).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "272b2c9e-a4a7-5ed6-8f8d-afccb0d806ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488381} {:answer/id #uuid "4b26b8fc-4484-5b18-a522-61d3df190b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488383} {:answer/id #uuid "64c1d8ba-687c-58d9-a1b1-afedc75c2ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488384} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0ff17a-224c-519e-bb32-5ca24c72a309" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488390} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1a4d4e-d4a9-55a1-8778-2c0a22a87f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{9}{2},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c1d8ba-687c-58d9-a1b1-afedc75c2ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488384} :answer-field/id #uuid "a58e450a-0fc0-578a-adc5-f85b28ec0484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488388}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "749e94e9-2ffe-5773-8eda-37c34a18a98e" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a1d3f3c077d921564732c5b27124a742f759e43dca9fb1cf9d8b931a24987dd0.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2 x}{3} -5$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2 x}{3} -5$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(-2) &= \\frac{3}{2}(x-(2)) \\\\ y-(-2) &= \\frac{3 x}{2} -3 \\\\ y &= \\frac{3 x}{2} -5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}y=-\\frac{2 x}{3} -5 \\\\ y=\\frac{3 x}{2} -5\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2 x}{3} -5 &= \\frac{3 x}{2} -5 \\\\ -\\frac{13 x}{6} &= 0 \\\\ x &= 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-5.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-5.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2 x}{3} -5$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is $(0,-5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488392 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058646a8-897c-5351-af83-134394fd95e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488379} {:answer/id #uuid "0d414122-f0da-51f3-9d93-0c7407b043f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488380} {:answer/id #uuid "4afcebdc-5704-5aa1-9833-83c262246e3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{9}{2},-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488382} {:answer/id #uuid "6ff2cc12-8660-5727-b18f-9ba7aed2ea2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488385} {:answer/id #uuid "8d94a87b-0937-5a65-bad7-29f5651e091a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "058646a8-897c-5351-af83-134394fd95e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488379} :answer-field/id #uuid "ade496b6-83fd-5b55-8e55-9fbb16591fb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186488389}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cef2e4e7-9752-58de-86dc-8f53c256eb68" :question/problem "![](images/c0/c0e534ede9457e7642697eea0b1f5b4e18454c69e233fb53570181538341fdb0.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2 x}{3} -6$ that is closest to the point $(2,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2 x}{3} -6$ and passes through the point $(2,-3).$\n\nThe given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\ y-(-3) &= \\frac{3}{2}(x-(2)) \\\\ y-(-3) &= \\frac{3 x}{2} -3 \\\\ y &= \\frac{3 x}{2} -6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}y=-\\frac{2 x}{3} -6 \\\\ y=\\frac{3 x}{2} -6\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2 x}{3} -6 &= \\frac{3 x}{2} -6 \\\\ -\\frac{13 x}{6} &= 0 \\\\ x &= 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-6.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-6.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-6).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2 x}{3} -6$ that is closest to the point $(2,-3)$ is $(0,-6).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175910 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175736 :question/id #uuid "4dd86f6d-270f-5942-b7cf-df53a0bf4ee4" :question/math-academy-id "e-14361" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(0,1)$ and is parallel to the line $y=\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 5$." :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 5\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{2}{3}x + 5\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nthat passes through the point $(0,1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{2}{3}(x - 0) \\\\\ny - 1 &= \\frac{2}{3}x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4dcbc520-40b5-5c08-ba56-be105c135240" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049716 :topic/math-academy-id 1240 :topic/title "Equations of Lines in Slope-Intercept Form"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050245 :topic/math-academy-id 837 :topic/title "Parallel Lines in the Coordinate Plane"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050635 :topic/math-academy-id 418 :topic/title "Equations of Lines in Point-Slope Form"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "20f45c71-4449-5cf6-9933-4eba8f82dccf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174747}] :question/id #uuid "10c44579-dc85-566b-8520-b63fbd992d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-215701" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4d7c284501bcfffef9626c71e3d768e3edd6b295f4228ee576f647e8cf6d4e9.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = \\frac{1}{2}x$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "857666d7-78f8-5db5-a976-bfbb90b20f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175079} {:answer/id #uuid "8e2be2ce-4b87-5f0d-a17c-01c13c08c89c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175080} {:answer/id #uuid "b25787de-333a-5901-aa2d-38ee9e940162" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175081} {:answer/id #uuid "b95b4071-0e5d-5e96-9a5e-f40faa0d6f76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175082} {:answer/id #uuid "352267a4-bc55-522d-81b9-edb8d934abe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175083}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cf2361b1-7b2e-57ca-9750-c20bd3fcd619" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186175078}] :question/id #uuid "ca38fd84-40dd-56ec-b024-482435371d75" :question/math-academy-id "q-44399" :question/problem "![](images/86/86d6eae2f6bec9ef31d993d09aa56cbca88432678e10e3fa8b4ef72b4058077b.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y =-x + 3$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481500 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec53f4e0-59fa-5407-885e-c992fc7f6850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec53f4e0-59fa-5407-885e-c992fc7f6850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481537} :answer-field/id #uuid "d498c16f-7247-5941-a672-80bd04343706" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481533}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "126edf55-54fb-5122-b14c-923ccea5da62" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f97a0ccabbbdd6db1b331deea33c80ab24f3beceb15754ac22d70e8aafb17b0c.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y =-\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$ that passes through the point $(-2,-3)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-3) &= -\\frac{2}{3}(x - (-2)) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{4}{3} + (-3) \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x - \\frac{13}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ca505fe-c114-51fc-8214-56e6f1ee6187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481510} {:answer/id #uuid "6d124f51-d60f-5d2b-bbb3-0d7bc296fb96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -3x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481514} {:answer/id #uuid "84ccd1b4-deca-57a1-be9b-c9d16ff3232f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481519} {:answer/id #uuid "e7706300-c8fd-5d50-9693-e1e42d23da1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -3x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481535} {:answer/id #uuid "f8076754-6a57-5d19-a479-ae27feb9fb8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 3x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7706300-c8fd-5d50-9693-e1e42d23da1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -3x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481535} :answer-field/id #uuid "1201a348-1556-5333-81fe-d1622269184e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481499}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a4b8abf-f619-531a-a8b5-8e24db57d662" :question/problem "![](images/ea/eaa82463dcdaad6bc1616b77c249a097483a8ca10afeda4bd7673896514b9efa.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = -3x + 1$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 1\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = -3x + 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n-3\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $-3$ that passes through the point $(0,-1)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= -3(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= -3x + (-1) \\\\\ny &= -3x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481505 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1605eca8-7e5d-57f4-bc3b-cada226d5c73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481501} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2550a3-5512-5a35-b0fd-9b57a7cb0a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481522} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d907de-6fc5-5a07-90c9-f562a7908aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481524} {:answer/id #uuid "b764ec71-0360-59c5-9664-95a1f3d21fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481525} {:answer/id #uuid "ded5822d-3d09-5994-861d-01de0f1c1bac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = -2x + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d907de-6fc5-5a07-90c9-f562a7908aeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x - 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481524} :answer-field/id #uuid "abe876a7-b557-5160-95bf-20e9357d1471" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481523}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b4f4747-b081-583d-87f5-e1f89a2c834b" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5baf024303863c69279bd1fb606a2bc8609dae35d5c5e1a9a998e9defaa0c621.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y = 2x - 3$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 2x - 3\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = 2x - 3\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n2\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $2$ that passes through the point $(0,-5)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-5) &= 2(x - (0)) \\\\\ny &= 2x + (-5) \\\\\ny &= 2x - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "281bcc22-655c-50c6-863e-af1edc8fb63b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "281bcc22-655c-50c6-863e-af1edc8fb63b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481503} :answer-field/id #uuid "c71a4191-1960-526c-94b7-cc9fc7172263" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481530}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "434017ea-0e77-5f9e-87dd-a52e593bb2fb" :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4951f9f511e3ddec793750a6562f20c5aed6cd161feedad6266e34ad95813a1.png)\n\nThe equation of the line $l_{1}$ is $y =\\frac{2}{3}x + 1$ and the line $l_{2}$ is parallel to $l_{1}$. What is the equation of $l_{2}$?$y =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{2}{3}x + 1\n$$\n\nThe slope of the line\n\n$$\ny = \\frac{2}{3}x + 1\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $\\frac{2}{3}$ that passes through the point $(6,3)$. Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (3) &= \\frac{2}{3}(x - (6)) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x - 4 + (3) \\\\\ny &= \\frac{2}{3}x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04badf